{{> Top}}<article class="prose">
<h1>Workloads</h1>
<p>Each workload is one part of a server&rsquo;s job. Every server must answer the same bytes, checked against a reference before it races (<a href="/method">Method</a>).</p>
{{?ready}}{{#groups}}<h2>{{ heading }}</h2>
<p>{{ note }}</p>
<div class="workloads">
{{#cards}}<section class="card"><h3>{{ title }} <code>{{ route }}</code></h3>
<p>{{ summary }}</p><span class="spec">{{ spec }}</span></section>
{{/cards}}</div>
{{/groups}}
<p>Each closed-loop workload: {{ warmup }}&nbsp;s warmup, {{ measure }}&nbsp;s measured, {{ rounds }} rounds, the median reported. Definitions: <a href="https://github.com/Eldhus/fourneau-dragrace/blob/main/race.json"><code>race.json</code></a>.</p>
{{/ready}}{{^ready}}<p class="pending">No race yet: the workloads show once the first has run.</p>
{{/ready}}
<h2>Closed loop</h2>
<p><a href="https://github.com/hatoo/oha">oha</a> holds <i>N</i> connections (each workload&rsquo;s above), each sending its next request when the last answer lands, so every server runs flat out at its own pace. By Little&rsquo;s law latency &asymp; <i>N</i> &divide; throughput: these are a saturated server&rsquo;s latencies, and they hide three things.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Coordinated omission.</b> A stall stalls the generator too; the requests users would have sent meanwhile never exist, so the tail is understated, most for servers that pause.</li>
<li><b>Latency at a given rate.</b> Each server runs at its own maximum, so no two latencies are at the same load.</li>
<li><b>The knee.</b> The loop sits at capacity throughout and never shows the climb to it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Open loop</h2>
<p>So each server then climbs a ladder{{?ready}} on {{ ladder }}{{/ready}}: fixed offered rates{{?ready}} at {{ shares }}{{/ready}} of its own closed-loop median, latency counted from when each request was due (<a href="https://github.com/giltene/wrk2">wrk2</a>&rsquo;s correction; oha <code>-q --latency-correction</code>). Steps, not a ramp, so each percentile belongs to one rate; plotted at absolute rates, so a slow server&rsquo;s easy half never passes for speed. The top step is past saturation on purpose: the queue grows for the whole step.</p>
<p>Pacing costs oha about half again the CPU of its closed loop, so at the fastest servers&rsquo; rates the loader can bind. A step with the loader {{ loader_limit }}% busy or more is drawn hollow.</p>

<h2>Caveats</h2>
<ul>
<li>The loader runs on a bigger droplet, its CPU recorded every round.</li>
<li>Echo on the larger classes can fill the network link first: the fast servers tie there (each round records the traffic).</li>
<li>Only 2xx answers count.</li>
</ul>
</article>
{{> Bottom}}
